var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"api/#API-Reference","page":"API Reference","text":"This page is generated directly from the source-code docstrings.\n\nUse it when you want exact function signatures, argument definitions, keyword defaults, and return conventions. For a narrative explanation of the workflows, start with the guide pages — Constructing Groups, Character Tables, and Representations.","title":"API Reference"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#FiniteGroups.FiniteGroup","page":"API Reference","text":"FiniteGroup(multab::AbstractMatrix{<:Integer}, name=\"Unnamed group\")\n\nA finite group defined by its multiplication table multab, where multab[i, j] is the index of the product of elements i and j (element 1 is the identity). Element inverses, conjugacy classes and class sizes are computed on construction. Validate an untrusted table first with check_group.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> FiniteGroup([1 2 3; 2 3 1; 3 1 2], \"C3\")   # cyclic group of order 3\nFinite group : C3\nGroup order  : 3\nClasses      : 3\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FiniteGroups.FiniteGroup"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#FiniteGroups.block_decomposition-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:AbstractMatrix}, FiniteGroups.AbstractFiniteGroup}","page":"API Reference","text":"block_decomposition(rep, group; R=true)\nblock_decomposition(rep, group_name::AbstractString; R=true)\nblock_decomposition(rep; R=true)\n\nDecompose a (reducible) representation rep of group into irreducible blocks, returning transformation matrices Sᵢ such that Sᵢ' * rep[g] * Sᵢ is irreducible. Pass R=true for real blocks. When no group is given it is reconstructed from rep (which must then be a faithful representation).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FiniteGroups.block_decomposition"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#FiniteGroups.charactertable-Tuple{FiniteGroups.AbstractFiniteGroup}","page":"API Reference","text":"charactertable(g::AbstractFiniteGroup; method=:dixon, tol=1e-7)\n\nReturn the character table of group g as a CharacterTable.\n\nmethod = :dixon (default) computes the table exactly over a finite field (see dixon); method = :burnside uses an alternative floating-point eigenvalue method that is subject to numerical tolerances.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FiniteGroups.charactertable"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#FiniteGroups.charactertable-Tuple{FiniteGroups.PointGroup}","page":"API Reference","text":"charactertable(g::PointGroup; method=:table, tol=1e-7)\n\nCharacter table of a point group. By default (method = :table) the standard table bundled with the package is returned, with conventional irrep labels. method = :burnside or :dixon recomputes it (see the AbstractFiniteGroup method).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FiniteGroups.charactertable"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#FiniteGroups.check_group-Tuple{Any}","page":"API Reference","text":"check_group(multab) -> Bool\n\nValidate that multab is a genuine group multiplication table: element 1 is the identity, the operation is associative, and every row and column is a permutation. Throws an informative error on the first violation; returns true otherwise.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FiniteGroups.check_group"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#FiniteGroups.check_proj_coeff-Tuple{FiniteGroups.AbstractFiniteGroup, AbstractVector{<:AbstractMatrix}, AbstractMatrix{<:Integer}, Integer}","page":"API Reference","text":"Check whether a linear representation of the cover group of g is indeed the  projective representation obeying the projective coefficients.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FiniteGroups.check_proj_coeff"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#FiniteGroups.check_real_rep-Tuple{FiniteGroups.AbstractFiniteGroup, Any}","page":"API Reference","text":"check_real_rep(g::AbstractFiniteGroup, χ)\n\nCheck whether a representation is real/complex/pseudo-real: +1 : Real   0 : Complex  -1 : Pseudo-real\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FiniteGroups.check_real_rep"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#FiniteGroups.chiral_proj_reps-Tuple{FiniteGroups.AbstractFiniteGroup, AbstractVector{<:Integer}}","page":"API Reference","text":"chiral_proj_reps(g, χ; R=true)\n\nRepresentation of g with a chiral operator S, which is represented by  Diagonal([1, …, 1, -1, …, -1]). \n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FiniteGroups.chiral_proj_reps"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#FiniteGroups.class-Tuple{FiniteGroups.AbstractFiniteGroup}","page":"API Reference","text":"class(g)    -> Vector{Vector{Int}}\nclass(g, i) -> Vector{Int}\n\nConjugacy classes of g: all classes, or the elements of class i. See also inclass and mult.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FiniteGroups.class"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#FiniteGroups.class_multab-Tuple{FiniteGroups.AbstractFiniteGroup}","page":"API Reference","text":"class_multab(multable, classes, class_vec)\n\nCompute class multiplicity:\n\nCᵢ × Cⱼ = hᵢⱼₖ Cₖ\n\nOutputs:\n\nh::Array{Int64, 3} : Class multiplicity.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FiniteGroups.class_multab"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#FiniteGroups.cover_group-Tuple{FiniteGroups.AbstractFiniteGroup, AbstractMatrix{<:Integer}, Integer}","page":"API Reference","text":"cover_group(g::AbstractFiniteGroup, coeff::AbstractMatrix{<:Integer}, p::Integer)\n\nConstruct the cover group of g, given the projective coefficient coeff.  The projective coefficient is unitary and the phase is module p.\n\nInputs:\n\ng     : Finite group. coeff : D(gᵢ) * D(gⱼ) = exp(i2π/p * coeff[i,j]) * D(gᵢ⋅gⱼ). p     : Module of coefficient.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FiniteGroups.cover_group"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#FiniteGroups.cycles-Tuple{Vararg{Union{Tuple, AbstractVector{<:Integer}}}}","page":"API Reference","text":"cycles(cyc...)\n\nConstruct a permutation from disjoint cycles, each given as a vector or tuple of point labels; e.g. cycles((1,2,3), (4,5)) is the permutation (1 2 3)(4 5). With no cycles the identity permutation is returned. See permutation for the two-column mapping form and permutationgroup to close a set of generators into a group.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FiniteGroups.cycles"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#FiniteGroups.dixon-Tuple{FiniteGroups.AbstractFiniteGroup, AbstractArray{<:Integer, 3}}","page":"API Reference","text":"dixon(g::AbstractFiniteGroup [, h]) -> Matrix{ComplexF64}\n\nCharacter table of g (rows = irreps, columns = conjugacy classes) via Dixon's method over a finite field. The hard step — the integer multiplicities of the roots of unity that make up each character value — is computed exactly in 𝔽ₚ, with no floating-point eigenvalue tolerance; those exact multiplicities are then assembled into the ComplexF64 entries returned here. h defaults to class_multab(g). This is the engine behind charactertable(g; method = :dixon).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FiniteGroups.dixon"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#FiniteGroups.equivalent_transform-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:AbstractMatrix}, AbstractVector{<:AbstractMatrix}}","page":"API Reference","text":"Return the transformation matrix between two equivalent irreducible representation rep1, rep2, satisfying:     rep2[i] = U⁻¹ * rep1[i] * U.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FiniteGroups.equivalent_transform"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#FiniteGroups.groupindex-Tuple{FiniteGroups.PointGroup}","page":"API Reference","text":"groupindex(g::PointGroup)\n\nIndex (1–32) of the point group g among the crystallographic point groups.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FiniteGroups.groupindex"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#FiniteGroups.inclass-Tuple{FiniteGroups.AbstractFiniteGroup, Any}","page":"API Reference","text":"inclass(g, i)\n\nIndex of the conjugacy class containing element i.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FiniteGroups.inclass"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#FiniteGroups.irreps-Tuple{FiniteGroups.AbstractFiniteGroup, AbstractVector}","page":"API Reference","text":"irreps(g::AbstractFiniteGroup; R=false)\nirreps(g::AbstractFiniteGroup, χ)\n\nCompute the irreducible representations of g.\n\nThe keyword form returns every irrep (one Vector of representation matrices, indexed by group element, per irrep); pass R=true for real (orthogonal) irreps instead of complex ones. Alternatively pass a single character vector χ (or a Characters/CharacterTable/character matrix) to project out only the corresponding irrep(s).\n\nSee also charactertable and block_decomposition.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FiniteGroups.irreps"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#FiniteGroups.matrix-Tuple{FiniteGroups.PointGroup}","page":"API Reference","text":"matrix(g::PointGroup[, i])\n\nRotation matrices of the point group g in the stored (crystal-axis) basis: the whole vector, or the i-th operation. See rotation for the Cartesian form, which differs for hexagonal groups.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FiniteGroups.matrix"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#FiniteGroups.mult-Tuple{FiniteGroups.AbstractFiniteGroup}","page":"API Reference","text":"mult(g)    -> Vector{Int}\nmult(g, i) -> Int\n\nSize of each conjugacy class, or of class i (the class multiplicity). This is not element multiplication — use g[i, j] for the product of elements i, j.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FiniteGroups.mult"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#FiniteGroups.name-Tuple{FiniteGroups.AbstractFiniteGroup}","page":"API Reference","text":"name(g) -> String\n\nName of group g; name(g, i) is the label of element/operation i.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FiniteGroups.name"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#FiniteGroups.operation-Tuple{FiniteGroups.PointGroup}","page":"API Reference","text":"operation(g)\noperation(g, i)\n\nSymmetry operations of g: the whole vector, or the i-th element. For a pointgroup these are operation-name strings (\"E\", \"C3\", …); for a permutationgroup they are permutations.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FiniteGroups.operation"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#FiniteGroups.oplus-Tuple{Vararg{AbstractMatrix}}","page":"API Reference","text":"Direct sum of matrices or representations.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FiniteGroups.oplus"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#FiniteGroups.order-Tuple{FiniteGroups.AbstractFiniteGroup}","page":"API Reference","text":"order(g)    -> Int\norder(g, i) -> Int\n\nOrder of the group g (number of elements), or the order of element i (the smallest k > 0 with iᵏ = 1).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FiniteGroups.order"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#FiniteGroups.permutation-Tuple{AbstractMatrix{<:Integer}}","page":"API Reference","text":"permutation(M::AbstractMatrix{<:Integer})\n\nConstruct a permutation from a two-column matrix M: each row [a b] means a ↦ b, and fixed points may be omitted. See cycles for disjoint-cycle notation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FiniteGroups.permutation"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#FiniteGroups.permutationgroup-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:FiniteGroups.Permutation}}","page":"API Reference","text":"permutationgroup(gens::AbstractVector{<:Permutation}; name=\"Unnamed Group\")\npermutationgroup(n::Integer)\n\nBuild a permutation group: either close a set of generating permutations (see cycles and permutation) into a full group, or, given an integer n, construct the symmetric group Sₙ.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> permutationgroup(4)          # S₄\nPermutation group : S4\nGroup order       : 24\nClasses           : 5\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FiniteGroups.permutationgroup"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#FiniteGroups.pointgroup-Tuple{Integer}","page":"API Reference","text":"pointgroup(i::Integer)\npointgroup(s::AbstractString)\n\nConstruct one of the 32 crystallographic point groups, selected by index 1:32 or by its Schoenflies symbol (e.g. \"Oh\", \"Td\", \"C3v\"). The index ordering follows the standard sequence \"C1\"=1, \"Ci\"=2, …, \"Oh\"=32.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> pointgroup(\"Oh\")\nPoint group : Oh\nGroup order : 48\nClasses     : 10\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FiniteGroups.pointgroup"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#FiniteGroups.proj_operator-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:AbstractMatrix}, AbstractVector{<:Number}, FiniteGroups.AbstractFiniteGroup}","page":"API Reference","text":"proj_operator(r, χ, g::AbstractFiniteGroup)\nproj_operator(r, χ)\n\nForm the group-algebra operator ∑ᵢ cᵢ * r[i] from a representation r (a vector of matrices, one per group element) and coefficients χ. Given a group g, χ may be a class function of length length(class(g)), in which case cᵢ = conj(χ[inclass(g, i)]), or a full length-order(g) vector with cᵢ = χ[i]. The two-argument form uses cᵢ = χ[i] directly. Projecting the regular representation with the rows of a character table is how irreps isolates each isotypic component.\n\nFor an abelian group the two lengths coincide (length(class(g)) == order(g)), so the class-function interpretation is taken; use the two-argument form when you need the full vector cᵢ = χ[i] without conjugation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FiniteGroups.proj_operator"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#FiniteGroups.proj_reps-Tuple{FiniteGroups.AbstractFiniteGroup, AbstractMatrix{<:Integer}, Integer}","page":"API Reference","text":"proj_reps(g, coeff, p; R, tol)\n\nCalculate the projective representation of g with coefficients coeff.\n\nInputs:\n\ng     : Finite group object. coeff : Coefficients represented by an integer matrix. p     : Modulus of the the integer. R     : Real or not, default = false. tol   : Tolerance, default = 1e-7.\n\nR=true realifies the irreps and keeps only those that still obey the scalar factor system; this is meaningful for real factor systems (e.g. p = 2, phases ±1). For a genuinely complex factor system (p > 2) the real form obeys a 2×2-block factor system rather than the scalar phase, so R=true may return nothing — use R=false there.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FiniteGroups.proj_reps"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#FiniteGroups.proj_to_irrep-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:AbstractMatrix}, AbstractVector{<:AbstractMatrix}}","page":"API Reference","text":"Transform matrix from a (reducible) representation to a given irrep.\n\nFor a representation D(g) and irrep d(g), find S that     S⁺⋅D(g)⋅S = d(g)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FiniteGroups.proj_to_irrep"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#FiniteGroups.proj_to_irrep-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:AbstractMatrix}, AbstractVector{<:AbstractVector}}","page":"API Reference","text":"Find a list of projection Sᵢ that     Sᵢ⁺⋅D(g)⋅Sᵢ = dᵢ(g), where {dᵢ} is a list of irreducible representations as an input.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FiniteGroups.proj_to_irrep"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#FiniteGroups.real_irreps-Tuple{FiniteGroups.AbstractFiniteGroup}","page":"API Reference","text":"real_irreps(g::AbstractFiniteGroup)\n\nGet all irreducible real representations of g.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FiniteGroups.real_irreps"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#FiniteGroups.real_rep-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:AbstractMatrix}}","page":"API Reference","text":"real_rep(r::AbstractVector{<:AbstractMatrix})\n\nReturn real representation for a complex-value real representation.\n\nFor a representation D(g), it is a real representation means that     S⋅D̄(g)⋅S⁺ = D(g) => Sᵀ = S, Find the sqaure root of S, i.e.,      S = W⋅W => W⋅D̄(t)⋅W⁺ = W⁺⋅D(g)⋅W The real representation is W⁺⋅D(g)⋅W. \n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FiniteGroups.real_rep"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#FiniteGroups.regular_rep-Tuple{FiniteGroups.AbstractFiniteGroup}","page":"API Reference","text":"regular_rep(g::AbstractFiniteGroup)\n\nReturn regular representation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FiniteGroups.regular_rep"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#FiniteGroups.repname-Tuple{FiniteGroups.PointGroup, Integer}","page":"API Reference","text":"repname(g::PointGroup, i::Integer)\nrepname(g::PointGroup, χ::AbstractVector)\n\nConventional Mulliken-style label of an irreducible representation of the point group g, selected by its index i or by its character vector χ.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FiniteGroups.repname"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#FiniteGroups.rotation-Tuple{FiniteGroups.PointGroup}","page":"API Reference","text":"rotation(g::PointGroup[, i])\n\nRotation matrices of the point group g in Cartesian coordinates: the whole vector, the i-th operation, or the operations indexed by a vector i. For hexagonal groups these differ from matrix by the change of basis to the hexagonal axes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FiniteGroups.rotation"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#FiniteGroups.transform_rep-Union{Tuple{Tv}, Tuple{Tr}, Tuple{Tu}, Tuple{AbstractMatrix{Tu}, AbstractVector{<:AbstractMatrix{Tr}}, AbstractMatrix{Tv}}} where {Tu, Tr, Tv}","page":"API Reference","text":"transform_rep(U, D1, V)\n\nTransform the basis of the representationc D1.\n\nThe inputs is U, D1, and V, the output is:     D₂(g) = U⋅D₁(g)⋅V\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FiniteGroups.transform_rep"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#FiniteGroups.unitary_rep-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:AbstractMatrix}}","page":"API Reference","text":"Convert a representation to unitary representation.\n\nConstruct the matrix H = ∑D⁺(g)D(g) = v⋅e⋅v⁺ = (X⋅X⁺)⁻¹ = (X⁻¹)⁺⋅X⁻¹      => X = v⋅(√e)⁻¹⋅v⁺, X⁻¹ = v⋅(√e)⋅v⁺ The new representation D'(g) := X⁻¹⋅D(g)⋅X is unitary:     D'⁺(g)⋅D'(g) = X⁺⋅D(g)⋅(X⁻¹)⁺⋅X⁻¹⋅D(g)⋅X = X⁺⋅H⋅X = I.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FiniteGroups.unitary_rep"},{"category":"section","location":"character-tables/#Character-Tables","page":"Character Tables","text":"charactertable is the main entry point. It returns a CharacterTable whose rows are the irreducible characters and whose columns are the conjugacy classes. Indexing the table gives entries, rows, or columns, and Matrix(ct) extracts the plain numeric array.\n\nusing FiniteGroups\n\ng = pointgroup(\"C4v\")\nct = charactertable(g)\n\nct[2, :]            # the second irrep's character, across the classes","title":"Character Tables"},{"category":"section","location":"character-tables/#Choosing-a-method","page":"Character Tables","text":"Three algorithms are available through the method keyword:\n\n:table — the standard table bundled with the package, with conventional irrep labels. This is the default for point groups, and is only available for them.\n:dixon — Dixon's method: the character values are recovered as exact integer combinations of roots of unity by working in a finite field mathbbF_p (no floating-point eigenvalue tolerance), then assembled into ComplexF64. This is the default for every group other than the point groups.\n:burnside — a floating-point method that simultaneously diagonalizes the class algebra. Entries are Float64 / ComplexF64 and carry small numerical error; pass method=:burnside to use it instead of the exact default.\n\nThe bundled and computed tables agree (up to the ordering of the irreps). Here is the same group via Dixon's exact method:\n\ncharactertable(g; method=:dixon)\n\nFor a group given only by its multiplication table, the bundled :table does not apply, but :dixon (default) and :burnside both do:\n\ns5 = permutationgroup(5)            # 7 classes ⇒ 7 irreducible characters\ncharactertable(s5; method=:dixon)\n\nThe standalone dixon function returns the raw exact character matrix (as a Matrix) without wrapping it in a CharacterTable, which is convenient when you only want the numbers.","title":"Choosing a method"},{"category":"section","location":"character-tables/#The-class-multiplication-table","page":"Character Tables","text":"Both solvers are driven by the class-algebra structure constants. These are exposed through class_multab, which returns the structure constants of the conjugacy-class algebra; you can use them to build custom solvers or to inspect the algebra directly.","title":"The class multiplication table"},{"category":"section","location":"getting-started/#Getting-Started","page":"Getting Started","text":"","title":"Getting Started"},{"category":"section","location":"getting-started/#Installation","page":"Getting Started","text":"FiniteGroups is registered in the General registry, so it installs the usual way:\n\nusing Pkg\nPkg.add(\"FiniteGroups\")\n\nor, from the Pkg REPL (press ]):\n\npkg> add FiniteGroups","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"getting-started/#A-first-character-table","page":"Getting Started","text":"using FiniteGroups\n\ng = pointgroup(\"C3v\")\ncharactertable(g)\n\nThe rows are the irreducible representations, labelled with their conventional Mulliken names, and the columns are the conjugacy classes. To get the plain numeric matrix, wrap the table in Matrix:\n\nMatrix(charactertable(g))","title":"A first character table"},{"category":"section","location":"getting-started/#Group-properties","page":"Getting Started","text":"However a group was built, it answers the same small set of queries:\n\n(; group_name=name(g), group_order=order(g),\n   num_classes=length(class(g)), class_sizes=mult(g))\n\nname, order — the group's name and order G.\nclass — the conjugacy classes, each a vector of element indices.\ninclass — the class index of a given element.\nmult — the size of each conjugacy class.","title":"Group properties"},{"category":"section","location":"getting-started/#Irreducible-representations","page":"Getting Started","text":"charactertable gives the characters; irreps gives the representation matrices themselves. Each entry of the returned vector is one irrep — itself a vector of matrices, one matrix per group element.\n\nrs = irreps(g)\n[size(r[1], 1) for r in rs]      # the dimension of each irrep\n\nSo C₃ᵥ has two one-dimensional irreps and one two-dimensional irrep, and rs[3][k] is the 2 times 2 matrix representing the k-th group element in the E representation.","title":"Irreducible representations"},{"category":"section","location":"getting-started/#Where-to-next","page":"Getting Started","text":"Constructing Groups — point groups, symmetric groups, and raw multiplication tables.\nCharacter Tables — the :table, :burnside, and :dixon methods.\nRepresentations — real/complex/projective representations and decomposition.","title":"Where to next"},{"category":"section","location":"representations/#Representations","page":"Representations","text":"","title":"Representations"},{"category":"section","location":"representations/#Irreducible-representations","page":"Representations","text":"irreps returns the irreducible matrix representations of a group: a vector with one entry per irrep, each entry itself a vector of matrices (one per group element). By default these are complex unitary representations.\n\nusing FiniteGroups\n\ng = pointgroup(\"D3\")\nrs = irreps(g)\n[size(r[1], 1) for r in rs]       # irrep dimensions — D₃ has irreps of dim 1, 1, 2\n\nThe matrices really are representations: rs[k][i] * rs[k][j] equals rs[k][m] whenever g_i g_j = g_m in the group.","title":"Irreducible representations"},{"category":"section","location":"representations/#Real-and-complex-realizations","page":"Representations","text":"Whether an irrep can be written with real matrices is governed by its Frobenius–Schur indicator: +1 for a real representation, -1 for a pseudo-real one, and 0 for a genuinely complex one. check_real_rep computes the indicator for a representation, real_rep realifies a single representation, and real_irreps returns all the irreps in real form:\n\nri = real_irreps(g)\n[eltype(r[1]) for r in ri]        # real-valued matrices","title":"Real and complex realizations"},{"category":"section","location":"representations/#The-regular-representation-and-projectors","page":"Representations","text":"regular_rep builds the (sparse, integer) regular representation; proj_operator forms the group-algebra projector sum_i c_i r_i from a character; and block_decomposition / proj_to_irrep decompose a given representation into its irreducible blocks. This is the machinery irreps itself uses to split the regular representation:\n\nreg    = regular_rep(g)                 # |G| sparse permutation matrices\nblocks = block_decomposition(reg, g)    # split into irreducible blocks","title":"The regular representation and projectors"},{"category":"section","location":"representations/#Projective-and-double-group-representations","page":"Representations","text":"For spin-1/2 and double-group problems the package builds projective representations, where group multiplication is realized only up to a phase (factor system). cover_group forms the relevant central extension, proj_reps computes the projective irreps for a given factor system, chiral_proj_reps handles the chiral case, and check_proj_coeff verifies a factor system. The chiral routine takes a one-dimensional real irrep (a character with χ[1] == 1) as its phase factor:\n\nct = charactertable(g)\n# use the one-dimensional A₂ irrep (row 2) as the chiral factor\nrreps = chiral_proj_reps(g, Int.(ct[2, :]); R=true)","title":"Projective and double-group representations"},{"category":"section","location":"representations/#Manipulating-representations","page":"Representations","text":"A few utilities round out the toolkit: transform_rep conjugates a representation by a change of basis, oplus forms the direct sum of representations, unitary_rep returns an equivalent unitary representation, and equivalent_transform finds the similarity transformation relating two equivalent representations.","title":"Manipulating representations"},{"category":"section","location":"groups/#Constructing-Groups","page":"Constructing Groups","text":"Three constructors cover the common cases. Whatever you build is an AbstractFiniteGroup and supports the same accessors — name, order, class, inclass, mult — and the same character-table and representation machinery.","title":"Constructing Groups"},{"category":"section","location":"groups/#Crystallographic-point-groups","page":"Constructing Groups","text":"pointgroup returns one of the 32 crystallographic point groups, by Schoenflies symbol or by index 1:32:\n\nusing FiniteGroups\n\ng = pointgroup(\"Oh\")        # full octahedral group\n(; group_name=name(g), group_order=order(g), num_classes=length(class(g)))\n\nThe valid symbols, in index order, are\n\nC1, Ci, C2, Cs, C2h, D2, C2v, D2h, C4, S4, C4h, D4, C4v, D2d, D4h,\nC3, C3i, D3, C3v, D3d, C6, C3h, C6h, D6, C6v, D3h, D6h, T, Th, O, Td, Oh\n\nPoint groups carry bundled reference data: the standard character table (used by charactertable unless you request another method), conventional irrep labels via repname, and the symmetry operations' rotation matrices via matrix (crystal-axis basis) and rotation (Cartesian). groupindex and operation recover a group's index and the list of operation symbols.","title":"Crystallographic point groups"},{"category":"section","location":"groups/#Symmetric-and-permutation-groups","page":"Constructing Groups","text":"permutationgroup builds the symmetric group S_n from an integer, or closes a set of generating permutations into a group. Permutations are written with cycles (disjoint-cycle notation) or permutation (an explicit a mapsto b table):\n\ns4 = permutationgroup(4)        # the symmetric group S₄\n\n# the same group, generated from a transposition and a 4-cycle\ng = permutationgroup([cycles((1, 2)), cycles((1, 2, 3, 4))], name=\"S4\")\norder(g)","title":"Symmetric and permutation groups"},{"category":"section","location":"groups/#From-a-multiplication-table","page":"Constructing Groups","text":"For any finite group, hand FiniteGroup its multiplication table — a matrix whose entry [i, j] is the index of g_i g_j — together with a name. Here is the cyclic group C_4, whose characters are genuinely complex:\n\nmultab = [1 2 3 4; 2 3 4 1; 3 4 1 2; 4 1 2 3]\nc4 = FiniteGroup(multab, \"C4\")\ncharactertable(c4)\n\ncheck_group validates that such a multiplication table really defines a group — a two-sided identity, associativity, and a permutation in every row and column. It takes the table itself:\n\ncheck_group(multab)","title":"From a multiplication table"},{"category":"section","location":"","page":"Overview","text":"<div style=\"text-align: center; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;\">\n  <img src=\"assets/logo.svg\" alt=\"FiniteGroups.jl\" width=\"160\"/>\n</div>","title":"Overview"},{"category":"section","location":"#FiniteGroups.jl","page":"Overview","text":"FiniteGroups.jl is a Julia package for the representation theory of finite groups. Given a group — a crystallographic point group, a symmetric group, or any group you hand it as a multiplication table — it computes the conjugacy classes, the character table, and explicit matrix representations, including the real and projective (double-group) representations that appear in condensed-matter and molecular-symmetry applications.\n\nThe package ships with reference data for the 32 crystallographic point groups: standard character tables, conventional Mulliken irrep labels, and the operations' rotation matrices. For those groups you get publication-style tables immediately. For an arbitrary group everything is computed from the multiplication table — an exact finite-field method (Dixon) by default, or a floating-point eigenvalue method (Burnside) as a faster alternative when exact character values are not required.","title":"FiniteGroups.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Manual-layout","page":"Overview","text":"Each guide page is written for human readers and emphasizes conventions, caveats, and runnable examples; the API reference is generated from the docstrings and lists exact signatures and defaults.\n\nGetting Started covers installation and your first character table and set of irreps.\nConstructing Groups shows the three ways to build a group: bundled point groups, symmetric/permutation groups, and a raw multiplication table.\nCharacter Tables explains charactertable and the three methods — the bundled :table, floating-point :burnside, and exact :dixon.\nRepresentations covers irreps, real vs complex realizations, the regular representation and projectors, decomposition into irreducible blocks, and projective / double-group representations.\nAPI Reference is the docstring-driven signature list.\n\nA reasonable reading order for a new user is Getting Started → Constructing Groups → Character Tables → Representations.","title":"Manual layout"},{"category":"section","location":"#A-minimal-example","page":"Overview","text":"Build the point group D₃ and look at its character table. Rows are irreducible representations (with conventional labels); columns are conjugacy classes.\n\nusing FiniteGroups\n\ng = pointgroup(\"D3\")\ncharactertable(g)\n\nThe same table can be obtained exactly, over a finite field, with Dixon's method — see Character Tables:\n\nusing FiniteGroups\n\ncharactertable(pointgroup(\"D3\"); method=:dixon)","title":"A minimal example"},{"category":"section","location":"#Package-scope","page":"Overview","text":"FiniteGroups.jl is focused on the finite, exact, and small-to-moderate regime: the 32 crystallographic point groups, symmetric groups, and groups small enough to store a full multiplication table and class-algebra. It is not a general-purpose computational group theory system (it does not do permutation group algorithms at GAP scale, presentations, or cohomology). What it does give you is conventional point-group data out of the box, two character-table algorithms — one fast, one exact — and explicit real, complex, and projective representation matrices, in a small codebase you can read end to end.","title":"Package scope"}]
}
